INT. SCHOOL BUS DEPOT - DISPATCH OFFICE - MORNING

A cluttered cinderblock office. A cracked wall clock, a coffee machine, a wall of key hooks. A grimy window overlooks a lot of yellow buses lined up like loaves. On the desk, an old intercom box with a blinking red light.

GLORIA PEPPER (50s, dispatcher, cat-eye glasses, a headset slung around her neck) sorts keys. MURRAY (60s, veteran driver, thermos the size of a fire extinguisher) reads the paper at the desk. PRESTON (20s, new driver, clip-on tie, too-clean uniform) hovers at the doorway, gripping a clipboard. YASMIN (30s, route supervisor, blazer, an energy drink) scrolls her tablet. All of them are slightly too loud.

GLORIA
Preston, route twelve. Don't hit anything.

PRESTON
I haven't hit anything yet.

MURRAY
Give it time.

YASMIN
Murray, be nice.

MURRAY
I am being nice. If I weren't nice, I'd tell him about the hill.

PRESTON
What hill?

YASMIN
There's no hill.

MURRAY
There's a hill.

The intercom crackles.

MRS. FEATHERSTONE (V.O.)
(intercom, crackling)
Gloria? Gloria, honey? It's Mrs. Featherstone from Pinecrest Elementary.

GLORIA
(pressing the button)
Good morning, Mrs. Featherstone.

MRS. FEATHERSTONE (V.O.)
(intercom)
The bus is here. But the children aren't on it.

A beat.

PRESTON
Wait, what?

GLORIA
Mrs. Featherstone, what do you mean the children aren't on it?

MRS. FEATHERSTONE (V.O.)
(intercom)
The bus is full of goats.

Silence. Murray lowers the paper.

MURRAY
(to himself)
The hill.

YASMIN
Who was driving bus nine?

All heads turn to Preston.

PRESTON
I swear I didn't do it.

GLORIA
Preston, get your keys.
